        "node": "V_RVOT",
        "wave": 0
      },
      "caption": "Rapid ventricular activity, often precipitated by ventricular tachycardia or ischaemia, breaks into many wavelets.",
      "ecg": "The trace becomes irregular."
    },
    {
      "id": "wavelets",
      "label": "Colliding wavelets",
      "at": {
        "node": "V_apex",
        "wave": 1
      },
      "caption": "Wavefronts arise from many sites, collide and die out in tissue that is still recovering. No single wave activates the whole ventricle.",
      "ecg": "Irregular deflections of varying size and shape: no QRS complexes."
    },
    {
      "id": "noflow",
      "label": "No coordinated beat",
      "at": {
        "node": "V_RV_free",
        "wave": 3
      },
      "caption": "The ventricle quivers instead of contracting, so there is no effective cardiac output. The patient loses consciousness within seconds.",
      "ecg": "A chaotic baseline with no identifiable QRS, ST segment or T wave."
    }
  ],
  "teaching_points": [
    "Ventricular fibrillation: rapid, totally chaotic ventricular rhythm, often precipitated by VT; fatal unless cardiopulmonary resuscitation with immediate defibrillation (slide 83).",
    "Reversible causes include ischaemia, electrolyte disturbance and the other 'Hs and Ts' (slide 77).",
    "Defibrillation depolarises all the tissue at once, so no excitable gaps remain for the wavelets."
  ]
